\description{
Models the called sighting category \strong{conditional on the true one}, so the
object being fitted is a row of the confusion matrix rather than a marginal
rate. Where \code{\link[=sdt_fit_behaviour]{sdt_fit_behaviour()}} answers "how often does a team call red",
this answers "given that this casualty is red, what does a team call, and
how far do teams differ in getting it wrong".
}
\section{The parameterisation}{
For each true category \code{k} the response has three levels and therefore two
free logits. They are fitted as a sequential pair:
\itemize{
\item \strong{hit} -- \verb{P(called = k | true = k)}, over every row with truth \code{k}.
\item \strong{split} -- \code{P(called = w1 | called != k, true = k)}, over the error
rows only, where \code{w1} is the more severe of the two remaining categories.
}

Each is a binomial mixed model

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{y ~ card + (1 | block) + (1 | group)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

This is an \strong{exact reparameterisation} of the three-category multinomial,
not an approximation to it: the multinomial likelihood factorises into the
two binomial likelihoods, so fitting them separately fits the multinomial
with the team random effects of the two logits held independent. The single
parameter the decomposition gives up is the correlation between a team's
accuracy and its error direction, and \code{joint = TRUE} estimates exactly that
(see below).
}

\section{Why the card term is load-bearing}{
Cards inside one true category are not alike. On the validation corpus red
card 14 was called correctly by 26\% of teams and 15 of the 30 red cards by
every team. Removing the card term does not shrink the team effect, it
\strong{erases} it: the fitted team SD goes to exactly 0 for red and for yellow,
against 0.544 and 0.639 with the card term, and the AIC is 473 and 137
worse. Every group saw nearly the same deck, so the spread of raw hit rates
across teams is \emph{narrower} than binomial noise around a single pooled
probability, and a model with one probability per category can only account
for that by setting the team variance to zero.

\code{card_effect} therefore defaults to \code{"fixed"} for the hit part. It takes
one value for both parts, or two -- \code{c("fixed", "none")} sets the hit part
then the split part -- or a named vector such as
\code{c(hit = "fixed", split = "random")}.

The split part defaults to \code{"none"}, and that is measured rather than
assumed. Its cells hold few error rows with a small minority (176, 124 and
529 rows with 10, 24 and 38 minority calls on this corpus); a card-fixed
split runs the yellow team SD up to 41 on the logit scale, is worse by AIC
than no card term for red, and leaves any card that no team ever miscalled
with no coefficient at all. It also costs little, because for red both
wrong answers are under-triage and for green both are over-triage: the
split moves cells \emph{inside} the confusion matrix without moving the
over- and under-triage rates.
}

\section{The ladder, and what gets skipped}{
A card term is attempted only where there are at least as many minority
events as card levels, and a fit is rejected if it is rank deficient, fails
to converge, or lands a random-effect SD above 5 on the logit scale --
which is separation, not a tendency. Each rejection demotes the cell one
rung down \code{"fixed"}, \code{"random"}, \code{"none"} and is recorded in the cell's
\code{demotions}. A cell that runs out of rungs, or that is constant, or that
has too few events or groups, appears in \verb{$skipped} with its reason and is
never dropped in silence. A fit that lands a zero team variance is \strong{kept}
and flagged \code{singular}: teams not differing is a finding, not a failure.
}

\section{The full multinomial}{
\code{joint = TRUE} stacks a category's two parts and fits them together with an
unstructured team covariance, which is the full multinomial mixed model for
that category. It is a \strong{diagnostic}: it measures the one parameter the
default gives up, and \code{\link[=sdt_simulate_triage]{sdt_simulate_triage()}} never reads it. Run on the
validation corpus it converged for green only -- correlation \code{-0.050}, and
a joint AIC equal to the sum of the two separate ones -- while red came
back rank deficient with a team correlation of 0.99 and yellow ran its
split SD to 33-40. The full multinomial does not fit reliably at this n,
and where it does fit it says the constraint costs nothing.
}

\section{The team term}{
Every cell is fitted \strong{twice}: once as above and once with \code{(1 | group)}
removed from exactly the same rows. \verb{$cells} carries \code{sd_group}, the null
fit's AIC, \code{delta_aic} and a likelihood-ratio test, so the necessity of the
team term is a measured claim per cell rather than a stated one. The null
fits are kept, and \code{\link[=sdt_simulate_triage]{sdt_simulate_triage()}} can simulate from them with
\code{team_effects = FALSE}.
}

